{"name":"Sage","hex":"#87ae73","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Sage","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname transferred from the nickname.","A male given name from English."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Sage"}],"entries":[{"title":"Salvia officinalis","blocks":[{"text":"Salvia officinalis, common sage or sage, is a perennial, evergreen subshrub, with woody stems, grayish leaves, and blue to purplish flowers. It is a member of the mint family (Lamiaceae) and native to the Mediterranean region, though it has been naturalized in many places throughout the world. It has a long history of culinary use, and in modern times it has been used as an ornamental garden plant. The common name \"sage\" is also used for closely related species and cultivars."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Salvia_officinalis0.jpg/330px-Salvia_officinalis0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salvia_officinalis","wikidata":"Q1111359"},{"title":"Salvia","blocks":[{"text":"Salvia is the largest genus of plants in the sage family Lamiaceae, with just under 1,000 species of shrubs, herbaceous perennials, and annuals. Within the Lamiaceae, Salvia is part of the tribe Mentheae within the subfamily Nepetoideae. One of several genera commonly referred to as sage, it includes two widely used herbs, Salvia officinalis and Salvia rosmarinus."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Salvia_officinalis0.jpg/330px-Salvia_officinalis0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salvia","wikidata":"Q157151"},{"title":"Sage (philosophy)","blocks":[{"text":"A sage, in classical philosophy, is someone who has attained wisdom. The term has also been used interchangeably with a 'good person', and a 'virtuous person'. Some of the earliest accounts of the sage begin with Empedocles' Sphairos. Horace describes the Sphairos as \"Completely within itself, well-rounded and spherical, so that nothing extraneous can adhere to it, because of its smooth and polished surface.\" Alternatively, the sage is one who lives \"according to an ideal which transcends the everyday.\""}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sage_(philosophy)","wikidata":"Q2624172"},{"title":"Sage (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Sage is a grey-green resembling that of dried sage leaves. As a quaternary color, it is an equal mix of the tertiary colors citron and slate. The hex RGB color value of the adjacent sage swatch is BCB88A. For decades, some military flight jackets were made in sage green."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Sage_iPhone_17.jpg/330px-Sage_iPhone_17.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sage_(color)","wikidata":"Q7399114"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Salvia_officinalis_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-126.jpg/330px-Salvia_officinalis_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-126.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Salvia_officinalis_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-126.jpg/330px-Salvia_officinalis_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-126.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Painting from Koehler's Medicinal Plants (1887)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKoeh-126.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Salvia_officinalis_Berggarten.JPG/250px-Salvia_officinalis_Berggarten.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Salvia_officinalis_Berggarten.JPG/330px-Salvia_officinalis